  "answer": "I cannot say whether narrowing would overcome the objection in your particular application. The supplied passages support the following distinctions, but do not explain when a specification limitation can overcome a place-based objection.\n\n**Act:** Section 17 defines a trade mark by its use, or intended use, to distinguish one person's goods or services from those of others. [TMA1995/s17]\n\n**Manual practice — descriptive but also a brand:** A descriptive element does not necessarily prevent use as a trade mark. However, in assessing capacity to distinguish, the consumer effect of that use is generally more relevant than the owner's intention. Calling the element your brand therefore does not, by itself, resolve that assessment. [TMM/Part19A/2#1~2; TMM/Part23/4/4/8~2]\n\n**Manual practice — geographical significance:** The Manual explains that the nature of particular goods or other circumstances may prevent a geographical name from carrying its usual geographical significance. Its explanation focuses on whether an honest competitor would think of using the name for those goods or in those circumstances. [TMM/Part22/15/15/1~1]\n\n**Limit of the supplied material:** That guidance makes the relationship between the name and the goods relevant; it does not establish that narrowing the goods or services will cure the objection. The passages do not provide the specification-amendment guidance needed to answer that part."
